        <p class="intro">
          Quick answers to common questions about cat behavior, health, feeding,
          and everyday care.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section class="faq" aria-label="Frequently asked questions about cats">
        <div class="example">
          <button class="question" type="button" aria-expanded="false" aria-controls="answer-why-purr">
            <span>Why do cats purr?</span>
            <span class="icon" aria-hidden="true">+</span>
          </button>
          <div class="details" id="answer-why-purr" hidden>
            <p>
              Cats often purr when they feel relaxed, but they may also purr when
              seeking comfort, recovering from stress, or communicating with people.
            </p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div class="example">
          <button class="question" type="button" aria-expanded="false" aria-controls="answer-feeding">
            <span>How often should I feed my cat?</span>
            <span class="icon" aria-hidden="true">+</span>
          </button>
          <div class="details" id="answer-feeding" hidden>
            <p>
              Most adult cats do well with two measured meals per day, though kittens,
              seniors, and cats with medical needs may require a different schedule.
            </p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div class="example">
          <button class="question" type="button" aria-expanded="false" aria-controls="answer-kneading">
            <span>What does kneading mean?</span>
            <span class="icon" aria-hidden="true">+</span>
          </button>
          <div class="details" id="answer-kneading" hidden>
            <p>
              Kneading is a common comfort behavior. Many cats press their paws into
              soft surfaces when they are content, settling down, or marking a cozy spot.
            </p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div class="example">
          <button class="question" type="button" aria-expanded="false" aria-controls="answer-scratching">
            <span>How can I reduce furniture scratching?</span>
            <span class="icon" aria-hidden="true">+</span>
          </button>
          <div class="details" id="answer-scratching" hidden>
            <p>
              Provide sturdy scratching posts near favorite furniture, reward your cat
              for using them, and keep claws trimmed. Scratching is natural, so redirect
              it rather than trying to stop it entirely.
            </p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div class="example">
          <button class="question" type="button" aria-expanded="false" aria-controls="answer-vet">
            <span>When should a cat see a veterinarian?</span>
            <span class="icon" aria-hidden="true">+</span>
          </button>
          <div class="details" id="answer-vet" hidden>
            <p>
              Schedule routine wellness visits at least once a year. Seek care promptly
              for appetite changes, breathing trouble, repeated vomiting, litter box
              changes, injury, or sudden behavior shifts.
            </p>
          </div>
        </div>
